**START HERE: [[ck-grav-prod-run]], the "GRID REVISED" section.** The production run is
designed and NOT launched: nx1 234 + the four stretch coefficients + `--gres=gpu:2`, giving
the user's requested **10 cells/H below the 1e-6 bar level** in ~90 h. The `ck_grav_prod`
directory still holds the old uniform nx1=200 input and a 1-GPU submit script -- it must be
rebuilt on the new grid, smoke-tested, and launched.

**The session's biggest correction: [[meshblock-decomposition-gpu]] was wrong at production
size.** Its "a second APU gains 1.4%" was measured on a 3.7x smaller grid; at nx1=234 two
GPUs give **1.81x (91% efficiency)** and every submit script in this campaign has been asking
for one of the node's two APUs. 1/2/4 ranks verified **bitwise identical**.

**Four errors of my own, all caught by measurement, all recorded:**
* I recommended a 0.55x cheaper grid on an **aggregated** cells/H metric that conflated the
  well-resolved observable with the under-resolved H2 structure. At the 1e-6 bar isobar the
  old grid was already at 9.7-17.2 cells/H. See [[dhj-grid-resolution-design]].
* I called the scale-height minimum "the H2 dissociation front" -- they are different radii
  (r/Rp 1.244 vs 1.280).
* I said the tide's source-only treatment broke well-balancing; **well-balancing is OFF** in
  this campaign, so there was nothing to break.
* I suggested rebalancing `f_stretch_theta` to recover cost once x1 limits; it cannot.

**Also worth keeping:** `bin_convert`/`athena_read` rebuild coordinates as
`linspace(xmin,xmax,nx+1)`, so **a stretched radial grid must have the map re-applied to
`x1v` in every analysis script**, exactly as theta already does.

**Cluster at sign-off:** `ck_limb` **11022482 is ~23 h into a 23:55 slot and will TIME OUT
imminently** -- it needs `sbatch resubmit_viper.sh` from `bench/ck_limb` to continue, and I
did NOT resubmit it because the open question is whether to reclaim that slot for
[[ck-grav-prod-run]] instead. Nothing is lost either way: `rst/` holds its restart files.
`ck_grav_size` 11043450 finished its slot and its dependency continuation **11046058 is now
running** -- that run has ANSWERED its question ([[ck-grav-size-run]]) and is the other slot
to reclaim.
