**The dhj campaign is CLOSED.** The six re-run arms landed and all six cleared tlim
3.934 rot -- including `rf.limoff`, the UNMODIFIED baseline that used to die at 0.370-0.685
rot. There was never a general-EOS blow-up: it was the data race fixed in **6e600f12**.
See [[dhj-ck-eos-blowup]] for the table and the full retraction list, and
`docs/HANDOFF_dhj_ck_eos.md` (**07a27135**), rewritten this session to match.

Retracted with it: "rt_de_max cures the general-EOS case" (it is still the right fix for
the *ideal* EOS + c-k NaN), "eos_h2=false cures it", and "the failure needs MHD". The nine
refuted hypotheses are moot rather than wrong.

Then, asked whether more bugs like 6e600f12 exist, I audited both hazard classes across
`src/`. **No second instance.** One real inconsistency found and fixed (**3429f59f**):
four well-balanced call sites in `MHD::CalculateFluxes` x2/x3 passed the x1 sweep's stale
`il,iu`. See [[mhd-fluxes-stale-x1-limits]], which also records what was checked and the
scan method, so the sweep need not be redone.

**Carry forward the lesson, not just the fix:** I claimed that bug caused a wrong flux and
a live race BEFORE measuring, and both claims were false -- pre-fix and post-fix are
bitwise identical over 5 replicates x 600 cycles. It is latent. I also deliberately did
NOT add a regression-test arm for it, because the arm would pass with and without the fix;
if a future session wants one, that is the reason it is missing.

Nothing is blocked. Natural next steps: open the PR for the correlated-k work
([[correlated-k-design]]), or resume [[inflight-nx64-timings]]. Still FOR THE USER, not
code: rotate the GitHub token embedded in the `origin` URL, and the completed `xe_*_e13`
runs carry the stellar-heating bug.

